          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Israel fundamentally denotes God’s covenant people, while its contextual scope may include Jacob, his ethnic descendants or nation, and the faithful people of God; Christian traditions dispute the precise relation between ethnic Israel and the Church.\",\"book_summary\":\"Israel is the covenant name given to Jacob and applied contextually at several scales: Jacob himself, his descendants, the nation, or the faithful people who prevail with God. Ethnic descent alone does not determine the referent.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Israel = Jacob and, principally, God’s covenant people, with context determining whether the people are understood ethnically, nationally, or spiritually.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Israel = the covenant name of Jacob extended contextually to the man, his descendants, the nation, or the faithful covenant people.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book encompasses the principal referents recognized across the consensus and explicitly adopts the consensus qualification that physical descent does not exhaust covenant identity. It leaves the disputed Church–ethnic Israel relationship less explicitly developed, but does not exclude either category.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book neither denies ethnic or national Israel nor restricts Israel exclusively to blood descent; this accords with the consensus’s broad core and its acknowledgment of contextual and theological variation.\",\"rationale\":\"After reducing both accounts to their minimum identification, both understand Israel as Jacob’s covenant name and as a designation for God’s covenant people whose exact scope depends on context. The book’s multiscalar formulation organizes cases already recognized in the responses rather than adding, excluding, or replacing a central referent. Its statement that blood alone is insufficient closely matches the consensus use of Romans 9:6.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Several responses foreground the Church as the spiritual or New Israel, whereas the book only says “the faithful people” and does not explicitly identify that group with the Church. Nevertheless, because the responses themselves deny a settled extension and recognize Jacob, ethnic descendants, and national Israel as valid contextual senses, this omission does not create a material disagreement.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
